Subject: Flocks of birds
Message-ID: <HMpmthiyTV4.0pNSyIp5AQAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/HMpmthiyTV4/m/0pNSyIp5AQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi folks,

I
have to run some distance sampling analysis on some old 1980s point-transect bird data. Aim is to measure the abundance of birds. I've run into an issue with duplicate data. It looks like the original observers have recorded flocks of birds, but each individual was entered as a separate record with the same time, location, and distance from observer. There is no information on the directionality of the observation, so it is possible these are independent observations.

How should I deal with these duplicate records? Should I fuzz the distance slightly since birds can't be in exactly the same space at the same time? Or does the number of individuals in a species become a covariate?

Thanks,

Cheryl

From u1fb54f491646a589 at groups.google.com  Tue Nov 24 09:36:37 2020
From: u1fb54f491646a589 at groups.google.com (Stephen Buckland)
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2020 09:36:37 +0000
Subject: RE: [distance-sampling] Flocks of birds
Message-ID: <HMpmthiyTV4.rkqHaAyPAQAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <HMpmthiyTV4.0pNSyIp5AQAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <HMpmthiyTV4.0pNSyIp5AQAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/HMpmthiyTV4/m/rkqHaAyPAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Cheryl, yes, in those days, typically people didn’t record groups.  You should be able to analyse the data ignoring this issue, but I would suggest carrying out 2 checks:  1. Select the bootstrap for variance estimation resampling points, and see if this makes much difference to variance estimates and confidence intervals.  (The bootstrap assumes independent points but individual detections at a single point are not assumed to be independent.  But the analytic estimates are pretty robust to non-independence, so this shouldn’t make much difference.  And 2. Try reanalysing the data by putting observations at the same time, location and distance into a group, and re-analyse the grouped data.  Again, you should get very similar results given the robustness of the methods, but this is worth checking.

No problem at all that you get multiple birds at a single location – perturbing distances a little has no advantages, and will add a little noise.  If however there is a lot of rounding in the data, you should consider grouping the distances before analysis, with group cutpoints avoiding favoured rounding distances.

Steve Buckland

